A||||15||85ZainoGT||I got the 2.8L but want more...?||||5||
Z||000000||85ZainoGT||08-29-2001||10:30 PM||||ok, is there any engine bigger than the 2.8 that has MINIMAL things that you have to do to get it going with the 85gt?  I want it to be simple as possible, is there an engine I can jus trade in and out, with nothing else, or small things left to finish it? I think you get what im sayin.. thanks in advance!||||reg||5
Z||000001||dennis_6||08-29-2001||10:34 PM||||I was looking into the same thing.  The best option seems to be a 3.4 engine.  You use most of the original equiptment if you find a pushrod one. <P>p.s. check out my post about 3.4 engine conversion.  I got alot info from others on the 3.4 conversion.<p>[This message has been edited by dennis_6 (edited 08-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||08-29-2001||11:01 PM||||The 3.4L p/r motors seem to be the big buzz word in the Fiero communities. When I began thinking about an engine build for my Formula, I was planning 3.4 route, and a few of my old posts referenced my planning stage of 3.4L. But after many calculations I decided the 3.4(or 3.1) was not the route I wanted.<P>I decided to buy a spare complete 2.8L to rebuild, and am in the process of that right now. By the time I am finished, I will easily have into this engine more than some of the 3.4L people have in theirs, but less than others(I figure I will be near or over $1,700 by the time I finish).<BR>It is not uncommon for the 2.8 with mild head work to push 160+hp, ask 1FST2M6, I THINK he has 165hp <B>at the wheels</B>! The 2.8 can be worked and tuned to give very acceptable performance. I have a stock 2.8 in my '86SE, and I just hope my rebuilt engine pulls as strongly as that beast, it is really very quick.<P>Just don't rule out the 2.8 because of displacement. I find it to be a good base to start from. Plus, no starter hole drilling, or other 3.4 issues when stock Fiero intake is used.||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||08-30-2001||01:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B><BR>Just don't rule out the 2.8 because of displacement. I find it to be a good base to start from. Plus, no starter hole drilling, or other 3.4 issues when stock Fiero intake is used.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>... Then you spin a bearing. again.  (sorry, I just don't like 2.8s. or the 2.5s either, infact I think every motor GM put into the fiero is pure crap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  but I've been burned by them, and a sample size of 3 cars is not significant.  I still don't like them.\<P>Everybody will stand up and yell 3.4 these days, but there are issues.  mostly in fuel delivery, you need to do a fair bit of work to get a driveable car and extract the extra power. Slapping bigger injectors into a stock ECU won't work terribly well. <P>The 3.4 block does use significantly larger main (and cam) bearings I belive, and does fix the oiling issues on the crank that all my 2.8s are infamous for.<P>If you are doing a rebuild, I recomend the best dam bearings you can find, seat them insanely well, and run Mobil 1 in that puppy. <P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000004||BOILERMAKER||08-30-2001||02:09 PM||||Everyone has a different opinion.  I personally have built up a 2.8 into a 3.2 and love it ... cost $1500.<P>I am in the midst of putting a 3800SC into another one and am SURE I will love it.  Cost ... well, I have to watch it so that BENGAL doesn't get mad at me.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LAMBO||08-30-2001||02:12 PM||||How 'bout a V8!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/caddymotor_1.jpg"> <BR>Bolts right up to your tranny. No engine adaptor kits. Plentiful in salvage yards for cheap$$$. 4.9s are 200hp and 275ft/lbs and you can put a 2m8 decal on the back. Fairly simple as far as swaps go (you don't have to cut anything up). Check out these websites for more info: <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/cadero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/cadero.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/index.htm</A> <BR>Todd||||reg||1
Z||000006||MinnGreenGT||08-30-2001||02:15 PM||||Everything I've read states that the 2.8 stock injectors can supply enough fuel for a 3.4L- I'd suggest an Adjustable Fuel Pressure regulator to give it the extra if you think it needs it. Gives you more control over your pressue and is easily adjustable.  I've got a High-Output 3.1L and would've done the 3.4 had it been readily available at the time.  I still want a 3800 S/C, but for now I'm enjoying the 3.1 quite a bit!<P>{Edit: And I'm getting more & more interested in the Caddy V8 above... hmmm, I've got an '84 SE....}<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">  <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 08-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||85ZainoGT||08-30-2001||04:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LAMBO:<BR><B>How 'bout a V8!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/caddymotor_1.jpg">  <BR>Bolts right up to your tranny. No engine adaptor kits. Plentiful in salvage yards for cheap$$$. 4.9s are 200hp and 275ft/lbs and you can put a 2m8 decal on the back. Fairly simple as far as swaps go (you don't have to cut anything up). Check out these websites for more info: <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/cadero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/cadero.htm</A>  <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/index.htm</A> <BR>Todd</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>so I could take a 4.9, set it in my car, and be done with it? nothing more?||||reg||1
Z||000008||p4n1c||08-30-2001||05:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B> ... Then you spin a bearing. again.  (sorry, I just don't like 2.8s. or the 2.5s either, infact I think every motor GM put into the fiero is pure crap   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  but I've been burned by them, and a sample size of 3 cars is not significant.  I still don't like them.\</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I run a 2.8 in my Cutlass, it is essentially the same engine as the Fiero, minus the distributor and turned around.  I've never spun a bearing and I have over 409,000kms(256,250 miles) on it.  The only complaint I have is restrictive exhaust and air intake.  But those can be fixed easily.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4<BR>*Under Restoration*||||reg||1
Z||000009||LAMBO||08-30-2001||05:31 PM||||No engine swap is that easy. There will be some front engine mounting issues that you would have to decide upon. Splicing in the harness, adapting the exhaust, rerouting fuel lines. Actually, if you take some time and check out the two websites I gave you, they pretty much explain everything that'll need to be done to make this swap work. The real attraction to this swap is the low cost, bang for your buck ratio.<BR>Todd||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||08-30-2001||09:45 PM||||The Camaro 3.4 swap is easy, I know I did it. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||87ECOsomething||08-30-2001||10:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR><B>The Camaro 3.4 swap is easy, I know I did it. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is true but, as someone dealing with this right now ... if you're planning to do the 3.4 swap and STOP THERE, you're in for a disappointment.<P>I had the Duke pulled from my '87 coupe and replaced with a remanufactured 3.4 V6 (bored out to a 3.6).  I figured going with the extra displacement would place me higher than stock 2.8 performance but below the bigger V6 and V8 conversions (exactly where I wanted to be).<P>That swap cost me $5200.  Guess what?  It's no better performing than a stock 2.8!  In fact, I actually had trouble running with <B>2M4 Dale</B>'s Duke!!!!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>The Fiero's intake system is not made to provide enough air for the extra displacement of the 3.4 V6.  Hooking the car up to a computer proved that - the car actually ran rich in the higher RPMs because it couldn't get enough air!<P>I'm also running with the stock 2.8 injectors.  Going to the recommended 3.4 (or 305) injectors only worsens my problem - not enough air coming in to mix with the extra fuel!<P>On top of that, I've heard (and seen) that the 3.4 doesn't want to rev as quickly as a 2.8 motor.  To correct that, I believe you can purchase lighter internals.  Of course, lighter internals = more $$$$$.<P>Since I spent so much money on the conversion and still have suspension and interior work to do, I can't justify spending any more dollars on the engine - unless it's to repair something.  So, what I'm left with is what I affectionately refer to as "The world's most expensive 2.8 conversion " because, in looks and performance, that's all I got!<P>Heed my warning:  don't do the 3.4 swap unless you have the knowledge, funds, time and desire to take it further!||||reg||4
Z||000012||fiero56||08-30-2001||11:25 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B> ... Then you spin a bearing. again.</B><P>2.8, 3.1, and 3.4 engines all use the same bottom end.<P>Rods are the same size, mains are the same size, 3.4L block is a beefed up version of 2.8L block. 3.1L is a 2.8 block. Only difference(other than balancing) between them is: 3.4 and 3.1 engines use 3.1L crank, and 2.8 uses 2.8 crank. All cranks are interchangeable in any block. If you want a different bottom end, you need something other than a 60* v-6.<P>I've got 180K miles on my '88 2.8 and it is getting loose. GM's Biggest mistake was using an externally balanced crank for earlier years.||||reg||1
Z||000013||GTMike||08-31-2001||04:31 AM||||87ECO  i have been reading up on the 3.4 push rod swap for a while. i'm planning on doing it this fall.  your story is the first bad one i have come across.   what did you do to spend over 5 grand.  i have found several used engine with 50k miles and a 3 month warrnty for 700 bucks.  (go to junkyarddog.com)   as far as running rich.. i would think it would be running lean cause your still useing 2.8 injectors.   when you say the 2.8 intake can't supply enough air are you talking about the manafolds. or the the actually air cleaner and hoses.    i think other then the engine  the only other expenses are drilling and taping the block for the starter. and modding an engine mount.  some of the engiens i found came with the fule rail and the injectors. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||dennis_6||08-31-2001||04:36 AM||||The caddy engine install looked pretty complicated to me.  I read alot about having to use custom adapters, and a caddy tranny.  I think I will stick with the 3.4||||reg||5
Z||000015||87ECO5||08-31-2001||07:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTMike:<BR><B>what did you do to spend over 5 grand. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had a shop do the job for me.  I don't have the bill in front of me so these numbers aren't exact but ... $1500 just to provide parts for the V6 (mostly intake and some other stuff, nothing is useable from the Duke), $2000 for the engine (without a core), $1700 for other parts and labor (a newer Isuzu 5-speed, free-flowing cat. convertor, 120 MPH gauges plus aux. gauges, many other things).<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTMike:<BR><B>are you talking about the manafolds.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm talking about every part that deals with airflow.  The intake system was designed to provide enough air for 2.8 liters of displacement (actually, it's closer to 3.0 but that's pushing it).  Likewise, the exhaust was designed to expel only what a "normal" 2.8 can exhale.  When you add another liter of capacity to this equation, you end up literally choking/smothering the engine.<P>Unfortunately, I didn't realize this before I wrote the check to the shop.  We'll call it a very expensive lesson ...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
